\begin{abstract}
Imitation learning has shown significant potential for advancing embodied intelligence. However, its efficacy is fundamentally constrained by the high cost of acquiring expert demonstrations and the compounding execution errors known as distribution shift. While digital twin technologies enable the low-cost generation of diverse synthetic data, existing methods typically rely on uniformly sampled simulation rollouts. This often leads to models that overfit to trivial behavioral patterns while failing to master the critical, high-difficulty task phases that are essential for robust manipulation. Conversely, Dataset Aggregation (DAgger) mitigates distribution shift through interactive human corrections; yet, its conventional "one-demonstration-per-trajectory" paradigm remains sample-inefficient and lacks sufficient trajectory diversity for complex tasks.
To address these issues, we propose Twin-DAgger, a framework that synergizes these paradigms by evolving the digital twin from a naive data source into an "explore-correct-augment" learning pipeline.
Specifically, Twin-DAgger utilizes DAgger as a real-world probe to identify critical failure points, which then serve as targeted anchors for digital twin augmentation. To further alleviate distribution shift, we introduce a dual-path task-focus strategy, which incorporates both spatial-aware and temporal-aware mechanisms, specifically designed to amplify the most challenging task boundaries identified during the DAgger process. Our Twin-DAgger pipeline enables the distillation of a singular human correction into a manifold of diverse, high-utility training samples. Notably, Twin-DAgger matches the performance of conventional real-world expert correction with only 10\% of the intervention cost, significantly outperforming uniform digital twin baselines under equivalent data budgets.



\keywords{robotics \and imitation learning \and digital twins}
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}


Imitation learning \cite{zitkovich2023rt} has emerged as a scalable paradigm for robot manipulation, enabling policies to acquire complex visuomotor skills directly from expert demonstrations. Recent advances have demonstrated remarkable success across diverse real-world tasks, particularly in long-horizon tasks \cite{chi2025diffusion,black2024pi_0} and high-precision operations \cite{zhao2023learning}. However, learned policies often struggle to inherit the full breadth of human expertise about the physical world. When deployed in out-of-distribution (OOD) scenarios unseen during training, these policies are highly susceptible to compounding execution errors \cite{zhang2024diffusion}. This phenomenon is known as distribution shift \cite{xucompliant} and often cascades into catastrophic task failure.


Dataset Aggregation (DAgger) \cite{ross2011reduction} mitigates distribution shift by introducing human corrections during deployment, but this approach faces significant practical hurdles. Conventional DAgger performs in real world, which remains highly inefficient \cite{xucompliant}, because each costly intervention yields only a single corrective trajectory. This one-to-one ratio between expert effort and data production is insufficient to capture the vast diversity of potential OOD failures. Furthermore, the high costs of expert labor and hardware maintenance make large-scale data collection through this method prohibitively expensive. Because corrections only occur when the robot happens to encounter a failure, there is no proactive mechanism to efficiently address all vulnerable regions of the task space, limiting the robot's ability to generalize to new OOD conditions.

One promising way to alleviate these constraints is through Digital Twin (DT) techniques \cite{yu2025real2render2real,lim2022real2sim2real}, which can generate synthetic training data at scale. Nevertheless, existing DT pipelines often rely on uniform augmentation strategies \cite{mandlekar2023mimicgen,robosplat2025} that treat every region of the task space as equally important. This approach does not adequately cover the specific OOD scenarios in which the distribution shift is more likely to occur, often neglecting the fragile boundaries of tasks where small perturbations lead to failure \cite{corrado2023guided}. Because the resulting training data are dominated by easy and already-solvable configurations, the policies remain unreliable in the difficult regions where robustness is most critical.

Our key observation \boxin{is only claimed if it is the key to solve the problem (and newly proposed by us). It seems here you are citing something already exists.} is that human interventions during robot learning carry far richer information than a single trajectory label because they reveal both where in the task space the policy is vulnerable and when along the execution those failures occur \boxin{Be careful about this claim. or add some citation if existing paper already mention this.}. Two fundamental patterns support this observation \boxin{If there are two, clearly list them as 1) ... 2) ... Now it's hard to find from the texts following.}. Spatially, policy performance is rarely uniform across the workspace. Even with randomized training data, specific failure-prone regions persist where slight environmental variations cause execution errors. These regions are far more valuable for learning than configurations the policy already handles well. Temporally, human corrections tend to be brief and decisive. Adjustments often involve a quick grasp correction or contact stabilization rather than a full-task takeover. These short windows mark the hardest moments in the execution timeline \cite{mandlekar2020human}. We argue that these signals should not be treated as byproducts of data collection, but rather as a guide for concentrating data generation on the specific regions and timesteps that need them most.

To this end, we propose \textbf{Twin-DAgger}, a framework that unifies the corrective signal of DAgger with the scalability of digital twins through an \textbf{``explore-correct-augment''} pipeline. Specifically, we first reconstruct high-fidelity task scenes in the digital twin by capturing real-world manipulation contexts. We then initiate the explore phase by running the DAgger process as a probe to expose policy weaknesses and locate failure boundaries in the real-world workspace. Through this process, we extract signals containing spatial and temporal annotations of high-failure-rate regions. Leveraging these signals, we introduce a \textbf{dual-axis priority sampling strategy} that integrates workspace success-rate mapping with trajectory segmentation based on intervention timing, thereby isolating high-risk spatial regions and critical temporal windows to pinpoint the precise boundaries of policy vulnerability. This strategy guides the augment phase, where we perform augmentation in the digital twin to concentrate diverse rollouts around these identified failure boundaries. Through this pipeline, a single human correction is expanded into a diverse set of high-utility training samples that target the policy's actual failure modes. Empirical evaluations demonstrate that Twin-DAgger outperforms both standard digital twin methods and conventional real-world DAgger while using only ten percent of the human intervention budget.

The primary contributions of this work are threefold: 
