\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\subsection{Imitation Learning}
\subsubsection{Behavior Cloning.} Behavior cloning learns visuomotor policies by directly mimicking expert demonstrations. ALOHA \cite{zhao2023learning} employs temporal action chunking with transformer architectures to enable precise bimanual manipulation on dexterous tasks. Diffusion Policy \cite{chi2025diffusion} reformulates action prediction as conditional denoising diffusion, effectively capturing multi-modal action distributions for complex manipulation. RISE \cite{fang2025airexo} integrates 2D semantic features from vision foundation models with 3D geometric representations through spatial alignment, generating actions via Transformer-based \cite{vaswani2017attention} decoder and diffusion-based \cite{ho2020denoising} heads. 
\subsubsection{Vision-Language-Action (VLA) Models.} Recent work has scaled up imitation learning by pre-training on large, heterogeneous robot datasets. The RT series \cite{brohan2023rt,zitkovich2023rt} demonstrate that pre-training on diverse multi-robot datasets (Open X-Embodiment) \cite{o2024open} enables strong zero-shot generalization to novel tasks and embodiments. OpenVLA \cite{kimopenvla} provides an open-source VLA architecture that combines Internet-scale vision-language data with robot demonstrations for rapid task adaptation. The $\pi$ series \cite{black2024pi_0,intelligence2025pi_} introduce flow matching-based VLA models designed for general-purpose manipulation across diverse robot platforms. Despite their generalization capabilities, these models still require massive amounts of diverse real-world data and remain susceptible to compounding errors in out-of-distribution conditions where training data is sparse.
\subsubsection{Interactive Learning and Dataset Aggregation.} To address distribution shift, DAgger \cite{ross2011reduction} proposes an iterative framework where expert corrections are collected on states visited by the learned policy, ensuring the policy learns to recover from its own mistakes. DMD \cite{zhang2024diffusion} combines diffusion-based policies with DAgger for eye-in-hand manipulation, demonstrating significant robustness improvements in contact-rich scenarios through interactive corrections. CR-DAgger \cite{xucompliant} introduces compliant control during human interventions to collect more informative demonstrations for contact-rich manipulation tasks. However, conventional DAgger methods suffer from a critical efficiency bottleneck: each costly human intervention produces only a single corrective trajectory, yielding insufficient trajectory diversity to cover the wide range of potential failure modes in complex manipulation tasks. Our work addresses this limitation by transforming each intervention into a manifold of diverse, high-utility training samples through targeted DT augmentation.

\subsection{Digital Twins}
\subsubsection{Digital Twin Construction and Sim-to-Real Transfer.}
DT technologies construct high-fidelity virtual replicas of real-world environments, enabling scalable generation of synthetic training data. RoboTwin \cite{mu2025robotwin} presents a procedural generation framework for synthesizing diverse task variations within digital twin environments, supporting robust bimanual policy training. TwinAligner \cite{fan2025twinaligner} addresses the visual-dynamic alignment challenge by reconstructing simulation environments that match both photorealistic appearance and physical dynamics of real-world scenes, enabling reliable sim-to-real policy transfer. Twin RL-VLA \cite{xu2026twinrl} leverages digital twins for reinforcement learning-based exploration, using simulation rollouts to refine VLA models before real-world deployment. RialTo \cite{torne2024reconciling} proposes iterative refinement of digital twin parameters through real-world feedback to progressively minimize the reality gap.
\subsubsection{Data Augmentation in Digital Twins.}
Recent work leverages digital twins as powerful engines to expand limited real-world datasets through synthetic data augmentation. RoboSplat \cite{robosplat2025} utilizes 3D Gaussian Splatting \cite{kerbl20233d} to construct photorealistic virtual environments, enabling robust one-shot manipulation by varying object poses and camera viewpoints. Similarly, MimicGen \cite{mandlekar2023mimicgen} automates the generation of diverse demonstrations via object-centric spatial and temporal transformations within simulation. Furthermore, Sim-and-Real Co-Training \cite{maddukuri2025simandreal} demonstrates that blending digital twin rollouts with real-world collections significantly bolsters policy generalization and robustness.
Despite these advances, these approaches often fail to adequately cover the critical failure boundaries where distribution shift is most acute, resulting in training data dominated by easily solvable configurations. Moreover, these methods remain decoupled from human expert feedback, missing the opportunity to use interactive corrections as a guide. 
