\section{Methodology}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Twin-DAgger framework}
The Twin-DAgger framework is designed to address the prohibitive cost of data acquisition and the lack of robustness at critical task boundaries in imitation learning by transforming sparse human corrections into density-aware, targeted synthetic data manifolds within a digital twin \boxin{This is a good sentence to summarize the uniqueness of Twin-DAgger, but it seems that it was not reflected in intro.}. As illustrated in Fig. \ref{fig:pipeline}, the system operates as an "explore-correct-augment" pipeline. The core idea is to treat each human-provided correction not merely as a single data point, but as a topological seed to anchor a manifold of synthetic but physically consistent trajectories.

Specifically, we first deploy a base policy (trained on initial teleoperation data) for real-world inference to actively identify failures. When OOD scenarios occur, a human expert provides high-quality corrective data through DAgger interventions. These real-world scenes and trajectories are then reconstructed within a digital twin to undergo our dual-path augmentation strategy. For the spatial path, we perform Focal-Scale Stratified Sampling based on a task difficulty map derived from success rates, where regions with different difficulty levels generate a corresponding number of augmented samples. For the temporal path, we expand the trajectories of the DAgger intervention segments by injecting localized perturbations at the intervention start to perform Temporal-Focal Trajectory Expansion. Through this Twin-DAgger framework, we obtain high-quality digital twin data that is both region-aware and temporal-aware, effectively bridging the gap between sparse expert corrections and robust policy deployment.

\subsection{DAgger-Based Data Acquisition and Simulated Augmentation}
In the implementation of the Twin-DAgger framework, we first developed a Virtual Reality (VR)-based interactive data acquisition interface for the efficient collection of corrective data targeting Out-of-Distribution (OOD) scenarios. During the deployment and inference of the base policy, an expert monitors the robot's operational status in real-time via a VR device. Upon observing anomalous behaviors—such as prolonged freezing, severe jittering, or significant deviation from the target trajectory—the expert can instantaneously switch from autonomous inference to teleoperation mode by pressing a specific button on the controller. Under this sparse correction mechanism, the expert is not required to demonstrate the entire task; instead, they only provide a short, critical corrective segment to steer the robot back to the expert manifold or a recoverable state. Once the task returns to the normal path, the expert can release control, allowing the model to resume autonomous inference. This design not only significantly reduces the expert's workload but also ensures that each collected trajectory serves as high-quality OOD corrective data specifically addressing the model's vulnerabilities.

To support subsequent digital twin replay and data augmentation, we utilize the ManiSkill  simulator \cite{ManiSkill3_24} to construct a virtual environment highly aligned with reality. Regarding robot modeling, we import the URDF model of the robot into the simulation and configure it as an articulation. By feeding the joint angle sequences recorded from the real robot into the controller to drive the respective links, we achieve smooth and consistent motion replay within the simulation space. For scene reconstruction, we reconstruct 3D meshes of the workspace and objects through multi-view scanning and integrate FoundationPose \cite{wen2024foundationpose} for object pose tracking, ensuring precise initial pose alignment between the simulated and real-world scenes. Furthermore, during the rendering of in-hand and third-person view images, we dynamically randomize the background far-field colors for each data segment. This effectively encourages the model to focus its visual attention on the task-relevant workspace and end-effector interaction details, thereby fully extracting high-quality Real2Sim value from the DAgger corrective data.

Upon completing data synchronization, we perform large-scale augmentation of the DAgger data within the simulation platform to further enhance data diversity. First, regarding environmental lighting, we configure various combinations of light sources, including ambient light, directional light, and point lights, obtaining visual data with diverse illumination characteristics through randomized parameters. For the core object-centric augmentation, we implement an Object-Centric Trajectory Warping method that maps the original expert trajectory $\tau$ to a randomly generated target object pose $P_{aug}$. \boxin{Use non-italic fonts for subscripts (like avg.) for indicating the meaning (not the index like $i$, $j$)} For each augmented segment, we randomly sample a translation offset $\Delta p = (\Delta x, \Delta y)$ and a rotation angle $\Delta \theta_z$ around the vertical axis within the operation plane, transforming the object's pose from its initial position $P_{init}$ to $P_{aug}$.

To ensure the motion continuity and physical feasibility of the robot during the warping process, we introduce a dynamic rotation center $c_t$ that varies with task progress. During the approach phase (pre-grasp), if the target object is translated by a distance $x$ along the vector $d$, we apply linear corrections to the $N$ trajectory frames preceding the grasp. Specifically, the $i$-th trajectory point is modified by adding an additional translation offset of $\frac{i}{N} \cdot x$ along direction $d$ to its original pose. This design ensures the gripper precisely reaches the augmented object's actual position at the $N$-th frame (the grasping moment) while effectively avoiding discontinuities at the trajectory's starting point. Simultaneously, the end-effector orientation undergoes a gradual transition from its initial state to the target rotation increment using Spherical Linear Interpolation (Slerp) \cite{shoemake1985animating}, achieving a smooth transition based on the current frame progress $t = \frac{i}{N}$.

During the transport phase (post-grasp), the trajectory gradually returns to the original delivery path to ensure the precision of the final placement. For all trajectory points following the grasp, a uniform spatial transformation $T_{aug}$—consistent with the object's displacement—is applied to maintain a fixed relative pose between the object and the end-effector, thereby ensuring a high task success rate. Additionally, to prevent joint flipping in the 7-DOF redundant robot during Inverse Kinematics (IK) computation, we adopt an iterative solving scheme that utilizes the previous frame's solution as the initial seed. For each point $x_t$ in the trajectory, the solver performs numerical optimization using the joint configuration $q_{t-1}$ from frame $t-1$ as the starting point: 
\begin{equation}
    q_t = \text{IK}(x_t, \text{initial\_qpos} = q_{t-1})
\end{equation}
This strategy enforces constraints on inter-frame joint displacement, resulting in highly smooth and physically executable trajectories in joint space. Through this implementation, we can generate a vast number of augmented samples from a single DAgger demonstration, significantly improving the acquisition and utilization efficiency of corrective data.

